the medicine thank you so much for being  with us just in terms of that prime  ministerial visit at to the north  earlier this week do you detect detector  a change in tone from this conservative  government when it comes to support for  the partner for the power house I think  it's been a remarkable change so for the  last three years under Teresa Mays  government I think that the progress has  probably been most generally sees  generously described as lackluster I  think the reality of what Boris Johnson  announced last week including the  funding and a commitment to fund the new  line between Leeds and Manchester which  we believe should come through the city  centre here in Bradford the great city  of Bradford to really transform and  unlock the economic potential that  currently isn't being taken advantage of  in the north is a completely new  approach from the government and I think  that particularly by putting Jay berry  round the cabinet table appointing those  like Ritchie sunic who were strong  supporters of northern infrastructure I  think we have also got a team around the  prime minister who can genuinely deliver  which we we didn't have previous to the  change of government but does it go far  enough I hear news this morning that a  hundred business leaders eart from the  north er are writing to the prime  minister with one key suggestion yeah  absolutely so on devolution we've been  writing today along with John heaps  who's the chairman of the auction  Building Society where I'm speaking to  you from but also business leaders like  Juergen Maya who runs Siemens Chris  Oglesby he runs Brentwood's a number of  our chambers of commerce like the chief  executive of the chamber in East  Lancashire these people have all come  together to send a clear message along  with those who run a lot of our our big  organizations supporting business like a  local enterprise partnerships they've  all come together here in the north to  say that devolution is a key step we  need to take because unless we unlock  the northern economy even if we improve  the infrastructure we won't have the  skills and talent our businesses need  and so if you look for instance here in  Bradford we don't have a directly  elected mayor with responsibility for  improving services here in Yorkshire  outside of the Sheffield City region we  don't have that in Cumbria or in  Lancashire or in  over further down in Cheshire in the  northwest we need to fix that problem so  that rather than only half the north  having a directly elected mayor we get  to the hundred percent figure which we  believe alongside that investment  infrastructure would deliver the vision  that George Osborne supported and made  happen when he was Chancellor and that  Jim O'Neill who sort of came up with the  intellectual ideas wanted to to achieve  because although he wanted to bring the  north together as a virtual city using  that new line which Boris mentioned we  need to make sure that those in Bradford  who want the jobs those talented young  people this is Britain's youngest city  have got the skills to take jobs whether  they be here in organisations like this  in financial services or across in  Manchester or across in Leeds the moment  they don't get the support with skills  because we haven't got that power here  in the north to influence the skill  system and they also don't have the  infrastructure we need both of those to  happen and I think under Boris Johnson  whatever people's views about the wider  government's policies we do have someone  who having been an ex-mayor being the  mayor of London previously is committed  to support that agenda it can given the  support that you are seeing from Boris  Johnson for the power house I mean why  is it impossible for these changes to be  made at a remove at a distance from  central government rather than at  through the process of devolution that  you're asking for I think the key point  is that if you want to spend for  instance 39 billion pounds building a  new railway I would accept that the  Treasury and central government might  have some role in that and then I think  the North though could play a much  bigger role along with the private  sector in being trusted to deliver that  project in a different way to how we  traditionally deliver infrastructure but  if you want to for instance improve  skills you can't do that from a desk in  Whitehall and what we've seen around the  adult education budget for instance  which is now being spent by a Tory mayor  in Teesside by Labour mayors in places  like Liverpool and Greater Manchester  they're doing a much better job with the  existing scarce resources so we want to  spend more money in the north but I  think when Richard leaves who is the  leader of Manchester who first pioneered  this agenda still very much part of the  team driving forward the Greater  Manchester economy he was really clear  but as well as getting that new  investment that we need in the north of  England to rebalance our economy it'll  be pretty silly if we didn't spend  better the money we've already got and  when you look for instance at how Andy  Burnham is challenging and improving  transport in Greater Manchester he's  building the B lines  a network of cycle lanes he's not just  doing what maybe somebody in Whitehall  would do and the response that from  communities from businesses is actually  that we think we're getting better  decisions through our directly elected  mayors and unfortunately a moment places  like here in Bradford and missing out  despite the fact that this is one of  those places but if we don't take  advantage of it the UK won't succeed  whatever happens with brexit nevermind  the north of England and this is in the  national interest  so I also believe for instance in other  parts of the country the devolution  could play a bigger role I've also would  be an admirer for instance on G Street  in the West Midlands who's doing a great  job with his economy so the north is  obviously the place where there are 15  million people where the government can  make the biggest difference but I also  think the decentralization of power  moving away from a Whitehall machine  which is fundamentally broken in terms  of making the right investments for our  country is the only way that we're going  to move forward as a country and it's  the only way that we're going to tackle  that underlying productivity gap because  we know that making poor decisions from  Whitehall is one of the reasons why  outside of London the southeast we don't  see the type of economy but we will need  to have if we're going to succeed in the  global race how convinced then or are  you of the merits of hs2 I think we  would be absolutely supportive hs2 and I  think that the northern business leaders  who have written this letter have also  many occasions lobbied for that and I  hope that Claire Fox gets and difficult  questions for me I think the only policy  is a no deal Breck sir and cancelling  hs2 from from the brexit party and as  they want to win votes the north of  England I think their economic  decision-making and rationale is pretty  pretty appalling I think that what we  would say about hs2 is it is more  valuable because it will be linked to  northern powerhouse rail so the the  train line that the Prime Minister back  last week between Leeds and Manchester  that's part of the wider network to get  you from Newcastle to Manchester Airport  from Hull to Liverpool that wider  network when you plug it into HS to will  really rebalance the economy and I think  that that challenge that we want people  to get around the north of England for  jobs we also want to be able to bring in  talent from slightly further afield and  a lot of people for instance to go to  Birmingham if you live in the north of  England in leaves or Bradford it's  almost impossible other than by getting  in a car to get to places like  Birmingham the transport networks to get  around and between our regions are  absolutely appalling and so in this part  of the world where we haven't built a  new railway east-to-west since the  Victorians I think it's perfectly right  that the country built a new line for  instance from Leeds to Birmingham and on  to London a new line from Manchester to  Birmingham and a new line between Leeds  and Manchester because if we do that we  will get those really transformative  economic benefits and I think a concern  for me is that despite the National  Infrastructure Commission saying we can  afford both we can also actually afford  Crossrail to if you do both those  projects within 1 point 2 percent of GDP  there are some people out there saying  we have to choose between be able to get  people for instance young people be able  to get from Bradford to Manchester for  work or people from Leeds and Bradford  being able to get to London or  Birmingham having the capacity we need  on that hs2 network I don't think anyone  in London had to choose between building  the new rail lines Paris the Channel  Tunnel rail link HS 1 to give it its  proper title or building cross rail  which I think when it opens will  demonstrate the real value for money  that it will bring for London taxpayers  who have partly contributed along with  government to make that scheme happen I  believe that in the north of England we  deserve both we deserve the ability to  be able to get people to work reliably  and quickly and we deserve better  connections to the rest of the country  and I think that when the government's  review of hs2 has been concluded I think  if we can get a delivery model that  integrates hs2 and building northern  powerhouse Rail together we'll be able  to save money but we'll also be able to  demonstrate that that combined Network  actually will be more valuable than  doing either hs2 or northern powerhouse  Rail on its own I think by building both  projects will deliver the vision that  George Osborne has which was that  connectivity can really unlock that  growth anyway Henry Morrison at many  thanks for joining us
 

